In article <HAIRSTON.90Dec24171606@henry.ece.cmu.edu>, hairston@henry.ece.cmu.edu (David Hairston) writes:
> a question:
> 
> if you are assured a certain dungeon given a certain seed, what is
> to prevent you from testing it until you get it right?  while this
> may be the pursuit of happiness for some, it just doesn't seem
> like nethack to me.  similarly, playing in wizard-mode doesn't have
> that same threatening edge as playing a normal game.

While it would be possible to play the same 
map over and over, I suspect that only the sort of people who cheat at 
solitaire would do so.  I think the people who would cheat at
competitive nethacking already cheat by copying save files.  Competitive
nethack would be fun for the rest of us, and probably doesn't open more
roads to cheating than already exist.

> is there an overwhelming reason the dungeons have to be identical
> to be compared?  there is enough randomness in the game that one
> could argue that advantages and disadvantages "average" out over
> the course of a game.
...You're joking right?  You get the same scores regardless of the 
number or location of shops and altars?  Your wizards do just as well
whether they start with a ring of conflict or a ring of adornment?

I find that the following almost completely change the temper of the game,
are determined entirely by luck, and are rare enough to lead to very 
anomalous scores when they happen:
(very minor spoilers follow)
wish generator or polymorph wand in first few levels
bones file from well-equipped character in first few levels
altar on first 5 levels
bookshop with intelligent character
shield/amulet of reflection 
spellbook of charm monster with intelligent character
...there are probably many others

The point is that these items make the game much more winnable.  Picking 
up  Mojo while still on level 5 is an advantage that other players would
have a great deal of difficulty overcoming!  Finding a bones pile 
containing a wand of polymorph and a ring of pmorph control can give you
a win without much effort.

> i think the bones exchange is far more interesting if it could
